From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: --cppheap-max-incremental-sweep-duration-ms (tunable Oilpan sweep slice)

Stock behaviour (V8 15.6.75.57)
-------------------------------
src/heap/cppgc-internal/sweeper.cc, Sweeper::SweeperImpl::IncrementalSweepTask:

  static constexpr auto kMaxSweepDuration =
      v8::base::TimeDelta::FromMilliseconds(5);

After a CppHeap (Oilpan) GC with incremental sweeping, Start() posts two
foreground tasks on the mutator (renderer main) thread: a low priority one
(TaskPriority::kUserVisible) immediately and a regular priority one
(kUserBlocking) after 100 ms. Each task run finalizes and sweeps pages for
up to kMaxSweepDuration and, if main-thread work remains, reposts itself
(low priority: immediately; regular priority: after 1 ms; the regular task
only re-arms its 100 ms timer as long as the low priority one keeps
running). Both priorities use the same 5 ms constant.
In a trace of the game this shows up as 2-5 back-to-back
CppGC.SweepInLowPriorityTask / CppGC.SweepInTask slices of almost exactly
5 ms after every major GC (every 5-13 s); at 1000 FPS each slice is a
multi-frame hitch.

Change
------
- New V8 flag --cppheap-max-incremental-sweep-duration-ms (int, default 5,
  defined next to the other cppheap_* flags). Values below 1 are treated
  as 1.
- cppgc must not include V8 headers (src/heap/cppgc-internal/DEPS has
  "-src" with only src/base, src/heap/base and the trace-event headers
  re-allowed), so the value is plumbed the way CppHeap already hands
  per-cycle sweeping options to cppgc: a new field
  SweepingConfig::max_incremental_sweep_duration (heap-config.h, default
  5 ms) that CppHeap::CompactAndSweep() fills from the flag when it builds
  the SweepingConfig for Sweeper::Start().
- IncrementalSweepTask::Run() reads the duration from the sweeper's stored
  config instead of the constant; the constant is removed (it had no other
  user).
- Standalone cppgc (cppgc::internal::Heap) and all tests build
  SweepingConfig with two or three initializers and therefore keep the
  5 ms default.
- flags.cc, ComputeFlagListHash(): the new flag is skipped, like the
  memory tuning flags above it. It does not affect generated code, and
  without this every change of the value would change
  ScriptCompiler::CachedDataVersionTag() and make V8 reject all existing
  JS/Wasm code caches (kFlagsMismatch), which would also disturb A/B runs.
  This hunk is independent of the rest and can be dropped.

With the flag at its default the task passes TimeDelta::FromMilliseconds(5)
exactly as before, and the flag hash is unchanged (default-valued flags are
never hashed).

How to use
----------
--js-flags reaches V8 in the Electron main process
(shell/browser/javascript_environment.cc) and is forwarded to renderers
(content RenderProcessHostImpl), which is where it matters:
  kcc.exe --js-flags="--cppheap-max-incremental-sweep-duration-ms=1"
or, in the main script before app 'ready':
  app.commandLine.appendSwitch('js-flags',
      '--cppheap-max-incremental-sweep-duration-ms=1')
(combine with any other V8 flags in the same --js-flags string, separated
by spaces).

Trade-off
---------
The amount of sweeping work is unchanged; it is cut into more, shorter
main-thread slices. Sweeping therefore finishes later: free-list memory
becomes available later (the allocator prefers growing the heap over
waiting, so peak Oilpan memory can be slightly higher), allocation slow
paths stay in "sweeping in progress" mode longer, and if the next GC
starts before sweeping finished the remainder is completed synchronously
inside that GC (Sweeper::FinishIfRunning). With GCs 5-13 s apart and
10-25 ms of total sweep work that last case should not occur.

Limits of the knob
- The deadline is only looked at after every 4th page per page list
  (DeadlineChecker::kInterval = 4, and a new checker is created for each
  space's list), and one page's finalizers cannot be interrupted, so a
  slice can overshoot a 1 ms budget. Expect "about 1 ms", not a hard cap.
- Low priority slices are reposted with no delay, so several short slices
  can still run back to back if the scheduler has nothing else queued; the
  renderer gets a chance to run a frame between them, which is the point.

Other fixed main-thread sweeping budgets (not changed)
- object-allocator.cc: allocation-triggered sweeping,
  SweepForAllocationIfRunning(..., TimeDelta::FromMicroseconds(500)), for
  both normal and large allocations; only after heap expansion fails does
  it sweep the space unbounded (TimeDelta::Max()) and then FinishIfRunning.
- cpp-heap.cc: CollectCustomSpaceStatisticsAtLastGCTask::kStepSizeMs =
  5 ms slices every 10 ms; only runs for Blink's detailed memory
  measurement (ThreadState::CollectNodeAndCssStatistics), not after normal
  GCs.
- sweeper.cc: the repost delays kDelayForRegularPrioritySweeping (1 ms),
  kDelayWhileConcurrentSweepingMakesProgress (5 ms) and
  kDelayWhileLowPrioritySweepingMakesProgress (100 ms) are delays, not
  budgets.
- There is no separate idle-task sweeper at this version; the low priority
  task is that mechanism and is covered by the new flag.

A/B
---
Same binary, with and without
  --js-flags="--cppheap-max-incremental-sweep-duration-ms=1" (try 1 and 2).
In a trace with the "cppgc" category, CppGC.SweepInTask and
CppGC.SweepInLowPriorityTask carry a max_duration_ms argument that must
show the configured value; compare the longest main-thread task and the
frame-time spike right after each major GC, and check that the time from
GC end to the last sweep slice stays well below the GC interval.

Platforms: all. Not compiled here.

Build and check status (2026-11-21)
  - Compiled without any change on Windows in the Electron 45.4.1 DCHECK
    build (dcheck_always_on).
  - test/v8-sweep-flag-test.js: --v8-options lists the flag (default 5);
    every CppGC.SweepInTask / SweepInLowPriorityTask slice in a trace
    carries max_duration_ms = 5 without the flag, and 1 or 2 with
    --cppheap-max-incremental-sweep-duration-ms=1 or 2 (slice p50 5.10,
    1.15 and 2.15 ms).
  - Not checked: the effect on frame times in a real page.
The "not compiled" / "not verified" notes above were written before the
build; they stay for the caveats they list. Details and raw logs:
patches/electron-45/RESULTS.md and results/.

